  "narrative": "This wave produces two new very large dataset-specific associations and clarifies the CDKN2A/MDM2 result. H118 is the largest new finding: TP53 loss versus TP53 intact shows a strongly positive signed association with dependency_USP7 for the registered weaker-dependency direction, estimate +0.396 with 95% interval [0.325, 0.467]. Since estimates are already signed, the raw TP53-loss minus intact difference is positive, i.e. less negative dependency and therefore weaker USP7 dependency. Relative to the USP7 dependency mean magnitude (~0.175), the lower bound is roughly 1.9 times the mean, far above the 10% clinical-significance threshold. This is mechanistically plausible because USP7 is closely tied to p53/MDM2 biology, and p53 loss could reduce dependency on USP7. I accept H118 and request independent validation because it is a new, precise, mechanistically important claim. H116 is also a new large effect: PTEN loss versus PTEN intact shows a strong positive signed association for stronger STAT3 dependency, estimate +0.267, interval [0.215, 0.319]. Relative to the STAT3 dependency mean magnitude (~0.282), the lower bound is about 76% of the mean, so the evidence supports a clinically meaningful direction. I accept H116 and keep it active for context checks; PTEN/STAT3 is biologically plausible but less directly supported by prior negative STAT3 tests than the p53-USP7 finding. H112 shows that the CDKN2A-loss association with stronger MDM2 dependency also appears among TP53-loss cells: signed estimate +0.111, interval [0.035, 0.186], with lower bound about 13% of the MDM2 mean. I accept H112 as a robust context-specific dataset association and close that stratum. H113 shows the CDKN2A-loss MDM2 association among TP53-intact and MTAP-intact cells: signed estimate +0.106, interval [0.063, 0.149], lower bound about 23%. I accept H113 and close it as evidence that the accepted H99 result is not simply due to MTAP co-deletion. However, H124, the direct TP53-loss interaction, is near zero: signed estimate +0.008, interval [-0.077, 0.093]. The point estimate does not support a clinically meaningful interaction, but the interval is wide enough to allow important interaction in either direction, so I leave H124 unresolved and active. This means the CDKN2A/MDM2 association may not be p14ARF/TP53-specific in this dataset, contrary to a simple mechanistic expectation. H114 tests MTAP loss among TP53-intact and CDKN2A-intact cells; the signed effect is small, +0.009, interval [-0.015, 0.033]. The upper bound slightly exceeds a 10% threshold, but the point estimate is near null and the interval includes no effect, so I leave it unresolved rather than accepting or rejecting it. H119 tests whether the p14ARF/CDKN2A axis also predicts USP7 dependency among TP53-intact cells; the signed effect is essentially null, +0.003, interval [-0.019, 0.025]. The upper bound is near threshold and the point is negligible, so H119 is unresolved and deferred rather than accepted. The RIT1 test is negative for the proposed compensatory mechanism: H121 rejects the hypothesis that NF1 loss among KRAS-wild-type cells strengthens RIT1 dependency; the signed estimate is -0.011 and the upper bound for the proposed direction is below the 10% threshold. The NF1/KRAS post-hoc observation remains suggestive but not acceptably precise: H123 is positive, +0.036, interval [0.003, 0.069], but the lower bound is far below the 10% threshold; H115 in adenocarcinoma includes null. Thus I keep H115 and H123 active/unresolved rather than accepting. EGFR amplification among PIK3CA-wild-type cells (H122) is in the expected direction but has a lower CI bound near null; BRCA2 loss for DHX9 (H120) is near null with an upper bound that still allows a meaningful effect. Both remain unresolved. Overall, the new accepted evidence strengthens a p53-related USP7 association and adds PTEN loss as a strong STAT3 dependency association, while showing CDKN2A/MDM2 is robust to MTAP-intact restriction and also present in the TP53-loss stratum. All conclusions remain dataset-specific associations, not causal claims or generalizable claims.",
  "research_notes": "Updated state. Accepted validated core: H7 SMARCA4 loss -> much stronger SMARCA2 dependency; H9 MSI-high -> much stronger WRN dependency; H19/H20/H26 KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency robust across contexts; H99 CDKN2A loss among TP53-intact -> stronger MDM2 dependency, validated. New accepted this wave: H112 CDKN2A loss among TP53-loss -> stronger MDM2 dependency, closed context; H113 CDKN2A loss among TP53-intact/MTAP-intact -> stronger MDM2 dependency, closed specificity context; H116 PTEN loss -> much stronger STAT3 dependency, active pending context; H118 TP53 loss -> much weaker USP7 dependency, active and now requested for independent validation. The CDKN2A/MDM2 effect is not p14ARF-specific: H112 supports effect in TP53-loss, H124 interaction near null but wide/unresolved, H113 supports MTAP-intact, H114 MTAP independent near null/unresolved. Negative/null: H121 NF1 loss does not support stronger RIT1 dependency in KRAS-wt; H119 CDKN2A loss does not support strong USP7 effect among TP53-intact; H120 BRCA2-DHX9 and H122 EGFR among PIK3CA-wt are unresolved near/threshold effects; H115/H123 NF1-KRAS reversed effect remains suggestive but not acceptably clinically precise. Next analyze should prioritize H118 validation response, H116 context checks, newly registered USP7 Avana context, STAT3 PTEN TP53-intact context, MDM2 CDKN2A among TP53-intact/MTAP-lost, plus existing H125/H126 and unresolved robustness interactions. All conclusions remain associations in the supplied dataset only.",
